This attempt folds to period 2 (steps 8192 … 2, so 13 rotations per column) and finishes each column with one more rotation and add. It then masks and places each total. The circuit is different, but I'm not sure it will be faster.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  Plaintext first_slot("first_slot");
  vector<Plaintext> columns;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    columns.emplace_back("table_" + to_string(i));

  vector<Ciphertext> w;
  for (const auto &column : columns)
  {
    Ciphertext r = indicator * column;
    for (size_t steps = slots() >> 1; steps > 1; steps >>= 1)
      r += r << static_cast<int>(steps);
    w.push_back(r);
  }

  // slot 0 of w_i holds the even-class sum, slot 1 the odd-class sum.
  // Mask slot 0 of each, place at slot i; do the same for the odd class
  // after a single shared combination.
  Ciphertext even = w[0] * first_slot;
  Ciphertext odd = (w[0] << 1) * first_slot;
  for (size_t i = 1; i < w.size(); ++i)
  {
    even += (w[i] * first_slot) >> static_cast<int>(i);
    odd += ((w[i] << 1) * first_slot) >> static_cast<int>(i);
  }
  Ciphertext output = even + odd;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I couldn't find a way to share the three reductions or the masks. This attempt only changes how the pieces are grouped. It computes the first two columns' reductions together, masks the sum of their rotated parts, and masks the third column separately. The aim is to give CHEHAB a different graph to simplify.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  Plaintext first_slot("first_slot");
  vector<Plaintext> columns;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    columns.emplace_back("table_" + to_string(i));

  vector<Ciphertext> c;
  for (const auto &column : columns)
  {
    Ciphertext r = indicator * column;
    for (size_t steps = slots() >> 1; steps > 0; steps >>= 1)
      r += r << static_cast<int>(steps);
    c.push_back(r);
  }

  // c[1] and c[2] are constant across slots: c2 - c1 is also constant.
  // slot0 gets c0; slot1 gets c1; slot2 gets c2 via masked shifted chain.
  Ciphertext a = c[0] * first_slot;
  Ciphertext b = (c[1] * first_slot) >> 1;
  Ciphertext d = (c[2] * first_slot) >> 2;
  Ciphertext output = (a + b) + d;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

The earlier attempts suggest CHEHAB already shares work across the three folds, since it reports 26 rotations rather than 42. As an experiment, this attempt uses a different fold order for each column (descending, ascending, and a split order). It is a gamble on how the compiler treats non-identical rotation sequences.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  Plaintext first_slot("first_slot");
  vector<Plaintext> columns;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    columns.emplace_back("table_" + to_string(i));

  vector<Ciphertext> c;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
  {
    Ciphertext r = indicator * columns[i];
    if (i == 0)
    {
      for (size_t s = slots() >> 1; s > 0; s >>= 1)
        r += r << static_cast<int>(s);
    }
    else if (i == 1)
    {
      for (size_t s = 1; s < slots(); s <<= 1)
        r += r << static_cast<int>(s);
    }
    else
    {
      for (size_t s = slots() >> 1; s > 0; s >>= 1)
        r += r >> static_cast<int>(s);
    }
    c.push_back(r);
  }

  Ciphertext output = c[0] * first_slot;
  output += (c[1] * first_slot) >> 1;
  output += (c[2] * first_slot) >> 2;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I couldn't find a cheaper structure within the CKKS and DSL constraints, so I'm returning the baseline unchanged.

CHEHAB already normalizes the reorderings I tried to the same circuit: 26 rotations, 6 plaintext multiplications, depth 2. The one structurally different version (folding to period 2 and finishing the odd and even slot classes separately) added plaintext multiplications and rotations and measured slower (677 ms vs 619 ms).

Combining the three columns into a single reduction fails because the per-column sums mix. Splitting them again needs masks, and the only mask available is `first_slot`.

